1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,264
A few lectures ago, a pass rate moved four points, ninety-two to eighty- eight, and

2
00:00:05,264 --> 00:00:08,047
I told you that alone tells you almost nothing.

3
00:00:08,047 --> 00:00:12,843
I also told you a much bigger move, in a much smaller bucket, five out of five to

4
00:00:12,843 --> 00:00:15,270
two out of five, deserved real attention.

5
00:00:15,270 --> 00:00:18,763
You might reasonably have asked: says who, and by how much?

6
00:00:18,763 --> 00:00:20,362
This lecture is the answer.

7
00:00:20,362 --> 00:00:24,980
Not a formula to memorise, a way of thinking about sample size that tells you,

8
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:28,296
roughly, when to trust a number and when to shrug at it.

9
00:00:28,296 --> 00:00:33,210
The short version: small samples lie to you constantly, in both directions, and the

10
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:36,230
only fix is knowing roughly how small is too small.

11
00:00:36,230 --> 00:00:39,604
This is also the last piece of mechanism in this section.

12
00:00:39,604 --> 00:00:44,341
Everything after this lecture is about judgement: reading results honestly, then

13
00:00:44,341 --> 00:00:45,051
closing out.

14
00:00:45,051 --> 00:00:47,597
This is the last new idea you need to hold.

15
00:00:48,147 --> 00:00:50,177
Forget golden sets for a second.

16
00:00:50,177 --> 00:00:54,997
Here's a coin that lands heads exactly half the time, always, no exceptions.

17
00:00:54,997 --> 00:00:59,373
Flip it five times, call heads a pass, and write down the percentage.

18
00:00:59,373 --> 00:01:01,085
Do that ten separate times.

19
00:01:01,085 --> 00:01:05,588
The top row is what you get: twenty percent one round, eighty the next.

20
00:01:05,588 --> 00:01:07,554
Nothing about the coin changed.

21
00:01:07,554 --> 00:01:09,711
It is identical every single time.

22
00:01:09,711 --> 00:01:14,848
What changed is that five flips is a small window, and small windows swing wildly

23
00:01:14,848 --> 00:01:17,638
even when nothing underneath them is moving.

24
00:01:17,638 --> 00:01:21,253
Now flip the same coin fifty times per round, ten rounds.

25
00:01:21,253 --> 00:01:26,327
The bottom row barely moves, mid-forties to mid-fifties, hugging fifty the whole

26
00:01:26,327 --> 00:01:26,581
way.

27
00:01:26,581 --> 00:01:27,215
Same coin.

28
00:01:27,215 --> 00:01:28,420
Same fifty percent.

29
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:33,747
The only thing that changed is the sample size, and look what it did to how much you

30
00:01:33,747 --> 00:01:35,587
can trust any single reading.

31
00:01:36,137 --> 00:01:41,013
Here's the practical version, and it's deliberately a rule of thumb, not something

32
00:01:41,013 --> 00:01:43,094
you'd hand a statistician as proof.

33
00:01:43,094 --> 00:01:48,089
If you want the real statistics behind it, a proper confidence interval, ask whoever

34
00:01:48,089 --> 00:01:53,024
owns your analytics for one; the number below is enough to decide whether that call

35
00:01:53,024 --> 00:01:53,976
is worth making.

36
00:01:53,976 --> 00:01:58,733
At five cases, a swing of up to roughly forty-five points in either direction is

37
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:01,408
well within what pure luck alone can produce.

38
00:02:01,408 --> 00:02:02,895
That's most of the scale.

39
00:02:02,895 --> 00:02:05,928
At five cases, almost nothing is provably real yet.

40
00:02:05,928 --> 00:02:10,744
At fifty, the size most of your buckets probably sit at, noise alone rarely moves

41
00:02:10,744 --> 00:02:12,885
you more than about fourteen points.

42
00:02:12,885 --> 00:02:17,582
That's exactly why four points, on the aggregate fifty, genuinely was closer to

43
00:02:17,582 --> 00:02:18,653
noise than signal.

44
00:02:18,653 --> 00:02:23,529
At a hundred or more, you're down to about ten points of ordinary wobble, which is

45
00:02:23,529 --> 00:02:28,405
where your bigger, common-path buckets will eventually sit if you keep growing the

46
00:02:28,405 --> 00:02:29,059
golden set.

47
00:02:29,609 --> 00:02:34,538
Let's go back to the number that started this whole section: adversarial, five out

48
00:02:34,538 --> 00:02:36,822
of five before, two out of five after.

49
00:02:36,822 --> 00:02:39,227
Sixty points of movement, at five cases.

50
00:02:39,227 --> 00:02:44,036
Look at the table: that's right at the edge of what five cases can even measure.

51
00:02:44,036 --> 00:02:48,725
It's a real, honest, uncomfortable answer, and it isn't the one anybody wants.

52
00:02:48,725 --> 00:02:53,654
You cannot yet prove that drop is real, and you cannot dismiss it as noise either.

53
00:02:53,654 --> 00:02:57,922
Both of those would be overclaiming what five data points can tell you.

54
00:02:57,922 --> 00:03:02,851
What you can responsibly do is exactly what an earlier lecture already told you to

55
00:03:02,851 --> 00:03:07,420
do when a bucket comes back thin: treat it as a warning, and go collect more

56
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:10,486
adversarial cases before you either panic or relax.

57
00:03:10,486 --> 00:03:14,513
Fifteen or twenty would let you say something with real confidence.

58
00:03:14,513 --> 00:03:16,557
Five can only make you suspicious.

59
00:03:17,107 --> 00:03:19,933
Notice what "significant" actually means here.

60
00:03:19,933 --> 00:03:22,145
Not "big" or "important" on its own.

61
00:03:22,145 --> 00:03:27,059
It's "bigger than luck alone would produce, at this sample size." The exact same

62
00:03:27,059 --> 00:03:31,851
ten-point swing can be meaningless at five cases and alarming at five hundred.

63
00:03:31,851 --> 00:03:36,888
The exercise takes thirty seconds: find your smallest bucket, look up its row, and

64
00:03:36,888 --> 00:03:41,372
now you have a number, not a feeling, for how much movement to shrug off.

65
00:03:41,372 --> 00:03:46,409
Everything past that number is worth five minutes of somebody's attention before a

66
00:03:46,409 --> 00:03:48,129
launch decision leans on it.

67
00:03:48,129 --> 00:03:52,737
This cuts both ways, and the direction people forget is the reassuring one.

68
00:03:52,737 --> 00:03:57,344
A bucket sitting dead flat release after release isn't necessarily healthy.

69
00:03:57,344 --> 00:04:02,443
At small sample sizes, it can just as easily mean you haven't gathered enough cases

70
00:04:02,443 --> 00:04:04,163
to see the problem move yet.

71
00:04:04,713 --> 00:04:09,476
Say the cost plainly, because it complicates something from two lectures ago.

72
00:04:09,476 --> 00:04:11,270
Fifty cases sounded complete.

73
00:04:11,270 --> 00:04:12,322
It isn't, evenly.

74
00:04:12,322 --> 00:04:16,281
Your twenty common-path cases give you real statistical footing.

75
00:04:16,281 --> 00:04:21,230
Your five adversarial cases give you a smoke detector, not a verdict: useful for

76
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:25,622
noticing something might be wrong, not enough to prove it on their own.

77
00:04:25,622 --> 00:04:30,819
Growing that bucket to something you can actually trust statistically costs the same

78
00:04:30,819 --> 00:04:35,644
thing everything in this section has cost: real hours, finding or writing more

79
00:04:35,644 --> 00:04:38,799
adversarial cases nobody was in a hurry to collect.

80
00:04:38,799 --> 00:04:41,521
That's not a flaw in the four-bucket design.

81
00:04:41,521 --> 00:04:46,223
It's the honest limit of building an instrument in an afternoon instead of a

82
00:04:46,223 --> 00:04:46,718
quarter.

83
00:04:46,718 --> 00:04:49,007
Treat it as a roadmap, not a failure.

84
00:04:49,007 --> 00:04:52,162
Fifty cases got you a working instrument this week.

85
00:04:52,162 --> 00:04:57,358
Twenty adversarial cases, added over the next few releases as you encounter or write

86
00:04:57,358 --> 00:05:02,555
them, is what turns your weakest smoke detector into something you can defend in the

87
00:05:02,555 --> 00:05:04,967
same room as the rest of the dashboard.

88
00:05:05,517 --> 00:05:07,320
One thing before you move on.

89
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:12,540
Find your smallest bucket, and note down the swing that's genuinely still just noise

90
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:17,574
at that size, so you have a number to check any future alarm against instead of a

91
00:05:17,574 --> 00:05:18,320
gut feeling.

92
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:23,292
Then label it honestly on the dashboard: anything under fifteen cases is a smoke

93
00:05:23,292 --> 00:05:28,015
detector, not a verdict, until you've grown it, so nobody in a launch review

94
00:05:28,015 --> 00:05:29,817
mistakes a warning for proof.

95
00:05:29,817 --> 00:05:34,789
Next lecture, the last idea before the recap: how to read an eval result without

96
00:05:34,789 --> 00:05:38,642
quietly fooling yourself, even once every number here is real.
